Answer: Step 1: remove the body of pay(). Java expects a semicolon after a method that has no body.

```java
abstract class Payment {
abstract void pay(int amount)       // no semicolon
}
```

Compilation error: error: ';' expected Step 2: add the semicolon but not the abstract keyword. A method with only a signature must be marked abstract.

```java
class Payment {
void pay(int amount);
}
```

Compilation error: error: missing method body, or declare abstract Step 3: add the abstract keyword to the method. Now the class must also be declared abstract.

```java
class Payment {
abstract void pay(int amount);
}
```

Compilation error: error: Payment is not abstract and does not override abstract method pay(int) in Payment Step 4: declare the class abstract.

```java
abstract class Payment {
abstract void pay(int amount);
}
```

Answer: The implementation of pay() is hidden in the child classes UPI, Card and Wallet, and the only thing visible in the parent class Payment is the method signature, which is the essential part according to Java.

```java
abstract class Payment {
abstract void pay(int amount);
}
class UPI extends Payment {
```

void pay(int amount) {

```java
System.out.println("Paid " + amount + " using UPI");
}
}
class Card extends Payment {
```

void pay(int amount) {

```java
System.out.println("Paid " + amount + " using Card");
}
}
class Wallet extends Payment {
```

void pay(int amount) {

```java
System.out.println("Paid " + amount + " using Wallet");
}
}
public class Main {
public static void main(String[] args) {
Payment p1 = new UPI();
Payment p2 = new Card();
Payment p3 = new Wallet();
p1.pay(500);
p2.pay(1000);
p3.pay(1500);
}
}
```

Output: Paid 500 using UPI Paid 1000 using Card Paid 1500 using Wallet Explanation: each child object runs its own pay() body. The Payment object itself is never created.

