Answer: The parent class Bird has a fly() method. An ostrich does not fly and an eagle flies high, so the children change the body. Because the children do not use the parent body, Bird keeps only the signature and is declared abstract.

```java
abstract class Bird {
abstract void fly();
}
class Ostrich extends Bird {
```

void fly() {

```java
System.out.println("Ostrich cannot fly");
}
}
class Eagle extends Bird {
```

void fly() {

```java
System.out.println("Eagle flies high");
}
}
public class Main {
public static void main(String[] args) {
Bird b1 = new Ostrich();
Bird b2 = new Eagle();
b1.fly();
b2.fly();
}
}
```

Output: Ostrich cannot fly Eagle flies high Explanation: new Bird() would give an error because Bird is abstract, so the objects of Ostrich and Eagle are created and the Bird reference points to them.

